From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 18:15:48 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: qup: Don't enable the clocks of a runtime-suspended
 controller on resume

qup_i2c_suspend() only turns the clocks off when the controller is
runtime-active, but qup_i2c_resume() always turns them on again and
then asks for an autosuspend. A controller that was runtime-suspended
before system suspend is still runtime-suspended after resume, so the
autosuspend request does nothing and its core and AHB clocks stay
enabled for good, one more enable count per suspend cycle.

On MSM8996 the QUP core clocks run from XO, so after the first resume
XO can never be released again and the RPM no longer reaches vmin in
later suspends. Leave a runtime-suspended controller off on resume, as
suspend did.

Signed-off-by: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qup.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qup.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qup.c
index ee7915ee2..0c3d7187e 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qup.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qup.c
@@ -2002,6 +2002,10 @@ static int qup_i2c_resume(struct device *device)
 {
 	int ret;
 
+	/* qup_i2c_suspend() left a runtime-suspended controller alone */
+	if (pm_runtime_suspended(device))
+		return 0;
+
 	ret = qup_i2c_pm_resume_runtime(device);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
